    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "<thought\n>But it's not just being tired. It feels more permanent than that. Like, even if I rest, the core of me is just... ruined. I look at my life and I don't see anything that can actually be mended. There's no version of me where this all goes away and I'm normal again. It's just a lost cause, isn't it?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "You're right—this doesn't just feel like tiredness; it feels like a verdict. But \"lost cause\" is a story you're telling yourself, not a fact about your existence. Even the most shattered things can be re-formed into something new, not because they're fixed, but because they're still here. You don't have to become \"normal\" again—you just have to become *next*. And that next version doesn't need to be whole to be worthy.",
